---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site";

const cases = [
  {
    title: "Multi-step form filling",
    problem:
      "Sign-up flows, vendor portals, and admin consoles change markup often enough to break brittle selector scripts.",
    approach:
      "Describe the outcome (“create a project named X and invite Y”). The agent re-snapshots between steps and recovers from stale refs.",
    keywords: "AI form filling, browser agent workflows",
  },
  {
    title: "Authenticated SaaS ops",
    problem:
      "Internal tools rarely expose complete APIs, and cloud browser farms struggle with corporate SSO cookies.",
    approach:
      "Run Browsergent on the Chrome profile where you are already logged in. The agent acts in-context without exporting session state to a third-party farm.",
    keywords: "authenticated browser automation, Chrome side panel agent",
  },
  {
    title: "Research across many pages",
    problem:
      "Competitive research and docs spelunking means reading, clicking, and compiling notes across a trail of URLs.",
    approach:
      "Give a research goal. The agent navigates, reads snapshots, and can work with attached session files for notes you want to keep.",
    keywords: "AI web research agent, page extraction",
  },
  {
    title: "QA smoke paths on real UI",
    problem:
      "Critical product paths regress visually or interaction-wise even when unit tests stay green.",
    approach:
      "Ask the agent to walk a checklist (“open billing, change plan, confirm modal”) and report failures with the same UI a user sees.",
    keywords: "AI QA agent, exploratory testing Chrome",
  },
  {
    title: "Data cleanup in admin UIs",
    problem:
      "One-off cleanup tasks are too expensive to script and too tedious to do manually at scale.",
    approach:
      "Pair plain-English instructions with skills for repeated patterns. Review the run; stop anytime.",
    keywords: "browser RPA alternative, open source browser agent",
  },
  {
    title: "Prototype agent product ideas",
    problem:
      "Teams evaluating “Claude Code for the browser” want a local, inspectable reference before building their own stack.",
    approach:
      "Browsergent’s run_js + page.* boundary is a concrete architecture to study, fork, and extend under MIT / Apache-2.0.",
    keywords: "open source AI browser agent, Claude Code browser",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Use cases — AI browser automation"
  description="Practical Browsergent use cases: AI form filling, authenticated SaaS ops, web research, QA smoke paths, and open-source browser agent prototyping."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Use cases", href: "/use-cases/" }]}
>
  <article class="container container-prose prose">
    <header class="doc-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Use cases</p>
      <h1>What to automate with an AI browser agent</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Browsergent is strongest when the UI is real, logged-in, and a bit messy —
        the situations where fixed Playwright scripts become expensive to maintain.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <p>
        These scenarios are a good fit for a <strong>Chrome AI agent</strong>
        that can see the page and iterate. They are a weaker fit for fully
        deterministic CI suites that should never freestyle.
      </p>
    </section>

    {cases.map((c, i) => (
      <section class="case" id={`case-${i + 1}`}>
        <h2>{c.title}</h2>
        <p><strong>Problem.</strong> {c.problem}</p>
        <p><strong>How Browsergent helps.</strong> {c.approach}</p>
        <p class="tags">{c.keywords}</p>
      </section>
    ))}

    <section>
      <h2>When not to use Browsergent</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You need bit-for-bit deterministic CI with no LLM variance.</li>
        <li>You cannot allow an agent near production credentials yet.</li>
        <li>You need Firefox/Safari parity today.</li>
        <li>You want a fully managed cloud browser with vendor SLAs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For architecture details, read the <a href="/docs/">docs</a>. To try it
        on your machine, <a href="/download/">download the Chrome extension</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-inline">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/download/">Download Browsergent</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/faq/">Read the FAQ</a>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .doc-head { margin: 3.5rem 0 2.5rem; }
  .doc-head h1 { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .case {
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .case h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .tags {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--fg-subtle);
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .cta-inline {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .prose section { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
